      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ k & 5 & k\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & k\\end{aligned}|)+((-5)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ k & k\\end{aligned}|)+((-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ k & 5\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (k)-(2)\\cdot (5)))+((-5)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (k)-(2)\\cdot (k)))+((-2)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (5)-(2)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot (2k-10))+((-5)\\cdot (-4k))+((-2)\\cdot (-2k-10))\\\\ &=(12k-60)+(20k)+(4k+20)\\\\ &=36k-40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36k-40&=0\\\\ 36k&=40\\\\ k&=\\frac{10}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{10}{9}.$",
