      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}k^{2} & 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((k^{2})\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}|)+((7)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((k^{2})\\cdot ((7)\\cdot (8)-(8)\\cdot (8)))+((-1)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (8)-(8)\\cdot (-3)))+((7)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (8)-(7)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((k^{2})\\cdot (-8))+((-1)\\cdot (8))+((7)\\cdot (5))\\\\ &=(-8k^{2})+(-8)+(35)\\\\ &=27-8k^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-8k^{2}&=0\\\\ -8k^{2}&=-27\\\\ k^{2}&=\\frac{27}{8}\\\\ k&=\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}$ or $k=\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}.$",
