      ":question/worked-solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=|\\begin{aligned}-8 & 1 & 5 \\\\ k & 8 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\\\ &=((-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}|)+((5)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 8 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}|)\\\\ &=((-8)\\cdot ((8)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (-2)))+((-1)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (-4)))+((5)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-2)-(8)\\cdot (-4)))\\\\ &=((-8)\\cdot (6))+((-1)\\cdot (12))+((5)\\cdot (32-2k))\\\\ &=(-48)+(-12)+(160-10k)\\\\ &=100-10k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100-10k&=0\\\\ -10k&=-100\\\\ k&=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=10.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
